(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-7-inflammatory-lung-disease-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Inflammatory Lung Diseases Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 7","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 7","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 71-year-old woman with exertional dyspnea, clubbing and bibasilar inspiratory crackles is diagnosed with idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis. Which chest film finding fits her disease?","choices":["Bilateral hilar adenopathy alone","Interstitial markings in the lower lung fields","Nodular opacities in the upper lung fields","Eggshell calcification of the hilar nodes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis the radiograph shows increased interstitial markings in the lower lung fields, with progressive fibrosis over several areas, matching her bibasilar crackles. Upper lung field nodules belong to coal and silica dust instead. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 27, 34, 45, 53, 63"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman with interstitial lung disease had a chest film 5 years ago showing fine reticular, ground-glass opacities. Her new film shows medium reticular opacities described as honeycombing. How should this change be classified?","choices":["A shift to nodular disease","Progression from early to late disease","Healing from active to inactive disease","Progression to coarse cystic disease"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Among reticular opacities, fine ground-glass change is an early finding and medium reticular change, honeycombing, is a late finding. Moving from ground glass to honeycombing over 5 years marks progression, consistent with fibrosis, which damages the alveoli permanently. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 5, 26"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man has had four separate bouts of cough and dyspnea over the past year, each clearing completely between bouts. Which onset pattern of interstitial lung disease does his course fit?","choices":["Acute","Subacute","Chronic","Episodic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Interstitial lung disease can present acutely over days to weeks, subacutely over weeks to months, chronically over months to years, or episodically. The episodic pattern of recurrent bouts is less common and can be seen with hypersensitivity pneumonitis, which makes his exposures worth reviewing. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman has taken nitrofurantoin for years to prevent recurrent urinary tract infections. Over several months she has developed progressive exertional dyspnea and a dry cough, with bibasilar inspiratory crackles and a restrictive pattern on pulmonary function testing. She has no occupational, bird or mold exposures. What is the most likely cause of her interstitial lung disease?","choices":["Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis","Hypersensitivity pneumonitis","Sarcoidosis","Drug-induced lung disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Nitrofurantoin is one of the treatment-related causes of interstitial lung disease, beside radiation, methotrexate, amiodarone and chemotherapeutics, and it is also named among drugs that cause pulmonary toxicity. Years of use followed by exertional dyspnea, dry cough, crackles and restriction fit a drug cause. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 12, 23, 24, 35, 40, 72"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man has spent 20 years cutting and polishing stone in a quarry. He now has progressive exertional dyspnea and a restrictive pattern on pulmonary function testing. How is his occupational exposure classified?","choices":["Inorganic dust","Organic dust","Fumes and gases","Treatment related"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Occupational and environmental exposures are grouped as inorganic dusts, organic dusts and fumes or gases. Stone work releases free crystalline silica, and pneumoconiosis is caused by inhaled coal dust or various inorganic or silicate dusts, so his exposure is an inorganic dust. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 12, 13, 50, 60, 65"},{"q":"A 41-year-old woman with acute respiratory distress syndrome after aspiration remains hypoxemic despite low tidal volume ventilation, careful positive end-expiratory pressure titration and early prone positioning. Her arterial oxygen pressure is 51 mm Hg on a fraction of inspired oxygen of 0.90. Which option is reserved for her situation?","choices":["High-frequency oscillatory ventilation","Inotropes for supranormal oxygen delivery","Liberal fluid resuscitation","Extracorporeal membrane oxygenation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Her ratio is 51 / 0.90, about 57 mm Hg, well under 100, so she has severe acute respiratory distress syndrome that is refractory to ventilation strategy and prone positioning. Extracorporeal membrane oxygenation is the salvage option reserved for severe, refractory cases. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 72, 73, 74"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man has interstitial lung disease, and a lung biopsy shows granulomatous inflammation with vasculitis. His cytoplasmic antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibody test is positive. Which disease does this result point to?","choices":["Hypersensitivity pneumonitis","Granulomatosis with polyangiitis","Sarcoidosis","Goodpasture syndrome"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A positive cytoplasmic antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibody points to granulomatosis with polyangiitis, one of the granulomatous vasculitides that produce interstitial lung disease with a granulomatous response. His biopsy showing granulomas with vasculitis fits that pairing. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 21, 25, 44, 69"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man has had pulmonary sarcoidosis for 15 years. His chest film now shows advanced fibrosis with honeycombing, hilar retraction and bullae. Which radiographic stage is this?","choices":["Stage I","Stage II","Stage III","Stage IV"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Stage IV is advanced fibrosis with evidence of honeycombing, hilar retraction, bullae, cysts and emphysema, which his film shows. About 20% of patients with lung involvement experience irreversible lung impairment from progressive fibrosis, bronchiectasis and cavitation. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 45, 48"},{"q":"A 38-year-old Black woman has a dry cough and dyspnea of insidious onset. She has lupus pernio (skin sores on the face), nontender lymphadenopathy and liver and spleen enlargement. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis","Sarcoidosis","Systemic lupus erythematosus","Hypersensitivity pneumonitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Lupus pernio, skin sores on the face, is the skin involvement of sarcoidosis, and a dry cough, insidious dyspnea, nontender lymphadenopathy and liver and spleen enlargement complete its multi-organ picture. Sarcoidosis also occurs more often in Black patients and in women. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 18, 25, 35, 39, 40, 42, 43"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man installed insulation in shipyards from his twenties until 15 years ago. He has exertional dyspnea with rare cough, fine bibasilar crackles and clubbing. Pulmonary function testing shows reduced vital capacity and total lung capacity, and his chest film shows pleural plaques with linear streaking at the bases. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Hypersensitivity pneumonitis","Asbestosis","Silicosis","Coal workers' pneumoconiosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Insulation and shipyard work are asbestos exposures, and patients usually seek attention about 15 years after exposure. Exertional dyspnea with rare cough, fine bibasilar crackles, clubbing, reduced vital capacity and total lung capacity, and pleural plaques, the most common film finding, fit asbestosis. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 53, 55, 57, 58, 60, 66"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man with acute respiratory distress syndrome from diffuse pneumonia is on low tidal volume ventilation. His arterial oxygen pressure is 80 mm Hg on a fraction of inspired oxygen of 0.50, and an echocardiogram has excluded cardiogenic edema. Which intervention decreases mortality at his severity?","choices":["Routine pulmonary artery catheter","Transfusion to a higher hemoglobin","Early prone positioning","High-frequency oscillatory ventilation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. His ratio is 80 / 0.50 = 160 mm Hg, moderate acute respiratory distress syndrome. Early prone positioning decreases mortality in moderate to severe cases, so he qualifies, alongside the low tidal volume ventilation he is already receiving. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 72, 73, 74"},{"q":"A 76-year-old man has had interstitial lung disease for 9 years, and his dyspnea now occurs at rest. An echocardiogram shows right heart failure. What does the right heart failure indicate about his lung disease?","choices":["It is now cardiogenic edema","It has entered an acute phase","It is at an advanced stage","It is at an early stage"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Right heart failure appears with advanced interstitial lung disease, and dyspnea that has spread from exertion to rest also marks progression. Early in the disease the examination may be normal, so these findings place him late in a long, chronic course. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 22, 23, 24, 73"},{"q":"A 48-year-old man has worked as a sandblaster for 20 years. He has dyspnea, cough, weight loss and fatigue with crackles on examination. His chest film shows nodular opacities, 1-10 mm across, throughout both lungs, and eggshell calcification of the periphery of the hilar lymph nodes. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Hypersensitivity pneumonitis","Silicosis","Sarcoidosis","Asbestosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Sandblasting is a free crystalline silica exposure, and silicosis shows silicotic nodules 1-10 mm across throughout the lungs with eggshell calcification of the periphery of the hilar lymph nodes. Dyspnea, cough, weight loss, fatigue and crackles fit its presentation. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 26, 44, 58, 60, 61, 63, 69"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman with sarcoidosis and progressive pulmonary lesions was started on oral corticosteroids but could not tolerate them because of side effects. Which medicine is a recognized alternative?","choices":["Oxygen therapy alone","Methotrexate","Restarting corticosteroids","Inhaled bronchodilator"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Immunosuppressive medications, most commonly methotrexate, azathioprine or infliximab, are used in patients who are intolerant of corticosteroids or have corticosteroid-refractory disease. Her progressive pulmonary lesions still need treatment, and her intolerance moves her to one of these. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 46, 47, 54, 71"},{"q":"A 41-year-old man with a dry cough and exertional dyspnea is being evaluated for interstitial lung disease. Which chest film finding would point toward sarcoidosis or silicosis rather than idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis?","choices":["Progressive fibrosis over several areas","Pleural plaques at the lung bases","Hilar and mediastinal lymphadenopathy","Increased markings in the lower lung fields"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hilar and mediastinal lymphadenopathy on a chest film points to sarcoidosis or silicosis. Sarcoidosis shows symmetric bilateral hilar and right paratracheal adenopathy, and silicosis can show eggshell calcification of the periphery of the hilar lymph nodes. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 26, 27, 34, 35, 44, 58, 63"},{"q":"A 44-year-old woman has interstitial lung disease with a restrictive pattern. She has no occupational exposures, takes no medicines and has no hilar adenopathy. Her clinician wants a laboratory test that screens for a collagen vascular disease as the cause. Which test fits?","choices":["Anti-glomerular basement membrane antibody","Angiotensin-converting enzyme level","Antinuclear antibody","Cytoplasmic antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibody"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A high antinuclear antibody points to the collagen vascular diseases, the connective tissue disorders such as lupus, rheumatoid arthritis, Sjögren's syndrome and systemic sclerosis that produce an inflammatory, fibrotic interstitial response. With no exposures or drugs, an autoimmune cause is the next to consider. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 18, 25, 44"},{"q":"A 49-year-old man with acute respiratory distress syndrome after severe pancreatitis is now hemodynamically stable on the ventilator. Which fluid strategy is preferred?","choices":["Conservative, restrictive fluids","Liberal fluids to support oxygen delivery","Inotropes for supranormal oxygen delivery","Fluids guided by a routine pulmonary artery catheter"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A conservative fluid protocol, targeting a central venous pressure under 4 mm Hg, is preferred, and restrictive fluids significantly increase ventilator-free days and shorten intensive care stays. His hemodynamic stability allows that restrictive approach. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"A 57-year-old coal miner with complicated coal workers' pneumoconiosis has progressive dyspnea. Which pulmonary function pattern, including the diffusing capacity of the lungs for carbon monoxide, is expected?","choices":["Restriction with low diffusing capacity","Obstruction with high diffusing capacity","Normal volumes, high diffusing capacity","Restriction with high diffusing capacity"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Coal workers' pneumoconiosis produces restrictive dysfunction with a reduced diffusing capacity of the lungs for carbon monoxide. Simple disease can leave testing unimpressive, but his complicated, symptomatic disease brings the restrictive pattern and impaired gas transfer. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 51, 53"},{"q":"A 63-year-old former shipyard worker undergoes lung biopsy, which shows ferruginous bodies (a histologic result of inorganic dust inhalation) within fibrotic interstitium. What does this finding suggest?","choices":["Significant asbestos exposure","Active sarcoidosis","Organic dust hypersensitivity","Established mesothelioma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A ferruginous body is a histologic finding in interstitial lung disease that results from inorganic dust inhalation, and in tissue it suggests significant asbestos exposure. His years in shipyards are a listed asbestos setting, with insulation, demolition and construction. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 25, 44, 55, 59, 70"},{"q":"A 35-year-old man with stable sarcoidosis is planning his yearly follow-up. He will have a physical examination, pulmonary function testing, a chest film, an electrocardiogram and a chemistry panel. Which referral completes his yearly evaluation?","choices":["Repeat transbronchial biopsy","Bronchoalveolar lavage","Lung transplant evaluation","Ophthalmologic evaluation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Patients with sarcoidosis require a yearly physical examination, pulmonary function testing, chest film, electrocardiogram, chemistry panel and ophthalmologic evaluation. The eye examination identifies and manages ocular inflammation, which matters because iritis and uveitis are indications for corticosteroids. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 34, 44, 47, 48, 54, 62"},{"q":"A 46-year-old woman has 1 year of dry cough and exertional dyspnea, bibasilar inspiratory crackles and a restrictive pattern. She has no exposures, medicines, connective tissue disease or sarcoidosis findings, and computed tomography shows fibrosis that does not settle the subtype of idiopathic interstitial pneumonia. What is the next step?","choices":["Lung biopsy to define the subtype","Diagnosis from the scan alone","Ophthalmology for yearly eye checks","Lung transplant evaluation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Idiopathic fibrosing interstitial pneumonia may require biopsy for diagnosis, and treatment plan and prognosis depend on the subtype diagnosed. The rule that spares biopsy applies to patients over 65 who meet all four criteria; she is 46 and her scan leaves the subtype open. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 32, 35, 48, 54, 71"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man with acute respiratory distress syndrome after severe trauma is stable on the ventilator, and an echocardiogram has already shown normal cardiac function. The team is planning his hemodynamic care. Which should be avoided?","choices":["Routine pulmonary artery catheter","Conservative fluid protocol","Transfusion at 7 g/dL","Sedatives and antipyretics"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Routine pulmonary artery catheters offer no clinical benefit in acute respiratory distress syndrome and are avoided. Cardiac function is assessed by echocardiography to exclude hydrostatic cardiogenic edema, and he has already had one showing normal function. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 73, 74"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man with no occupational, drug or connective tissue disease history has 2 years of insidious dry cough and exertional dyspnea. He has bibasilar inspiratory crackles, pulmonary function testing shows a restrictive pattern, chest films over several years show progressive fibrosis, and high-resolution computed tomography shows diffuse, patchy fibrosis with pleural-based honeycombing. How should the diagnosis be established?","choices":["Clinically, without a lung biopsy","By surgical lung biopsy","By serum antibody testing","By transbronchial lung biopsy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Over age 65, idiopathic disease with inspiratory crackles, restrictive physiology, radiographic progressive fibrosis over several years and diffuse, patchy fibrosis with pleural-based honeycombing on high-resolution computed tomography give idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis with 90% confidence. Such patients do not need surgical lung biopsy. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 32, 33, 34, 35, 44, 69"},{"q":"A 47-year-old farmer recovered from acute farmer's lung after a month away from work, and he plans to return to the farm. During his illness he used oxygen, inhaled bronchodilators and pain medication. Which should he avoid?","choices":["Inhaled bronchodilators","Supplemental oxygen","Pain medication","Handling moldy hay"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Treatment of hypersensitivity pneumonitis is to identify the responsible agent and avoid it, and moldy hay is the source of farmer's lung. Continued exposure may lead to a worsened disease state, while symptoms caught early can be reversible. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 65, 69, 71"},{"q":"A 55-year-old woman is leaving the hospital after acute respiratory distress syndrome from pneumonia. She asks how long her lung function will take to recover. Which answer is accurate?","choices":["About 1 month","About 3 months","About 6 to 12 months","About 36 months"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Lung function generally recovers over 6 to 12 months after acute respiratory distress syndrome, though functional deficits often remain, with residual diffusion deficits persisting. Physical sequelae after intensive care, such as muscle wasting, weakness and fatigue, also need attention. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 72, 74"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman with interstitial lung disease has a computed tomography scan showing established fibrosis with honeycombing. She asks whether the scarring will heal once the inflammation settles. Which response is accurate?","choices":["It heals once inflammation settles","It dissolves with corticosteroids","It clears with breathing exercises","The scarring is permanent"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Fibrosis is the lung's response to an inflammatory source: alveolitis drives fibroblasts to lay down collagen, forming permanent scar tissue that damages the alveoli, stiffens the lung and impairs gas exchange. Honeycombing is a late finding, so her scarring is established and will not heal. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 4, 5, 6, 26, 65"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man has acute bird fancier's lung from the pigeons he keeps at home, and his symptoms are settling. He asks whether he can keep the birds if he takes medicine whenever symptoms flare. Which counsel is accurate?","choices":["Continued exposure may worsen his disease","Medicine makes ongoing exposure safe","His lung damage is already permanent","His symptoms come from asthma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Prompt diagnosis is key so the patient can be removed from the irritating environment: symptoms can be reversible if caught early, but continued exposure may lead to a worsened disease state. Treatment starts with identifying and avoiding the agent, here his birds. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 65, 69, 71"},{"q":"A 30-year-old man has started work in building demolition, where he handles old asbestos insulation. He asks how long after exposure asbestos lung disease usually leads people to seek care. Which answer is accurate?","choices":["About 1 year after exposure","About 5 years after exposure","About 15 years after exposure","About 40 years after exposure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Asbestosis develops over 10-20 years, and patients usually seek medical attention about 15 years after exposure. That long delay is why an exposure history matters in an older patient with dyspnea, and why a young worker's risk starts with exposure he has now. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 55, 66"},{"q":"A 57-year-old woman with acute respiratory distress syndrome from sepsis has a hemoglobin of 8.2 g/dL (normal 12.0-15.5) and no active bleeding. Her family asks that she be transfused to bring the value back to normal. Which should be avoided?","choices":["Early prone positioning","Transfusing if it falls to 7 g/dL","Transfusing to a normal hemoglobin","Conservative fluid protocol"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In acute respiratory distress syndrome the transfusion threshold is a hemoglobin of 7 g/dL, and higher hemoglobin targets do not improve patient outcomes. Her 8.2 g/dL is low against the 12.0-15.5 range but above the threshold, so transfusing toward normal offers no outcome benefit. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"A 32-year-old woman newly diagnosed with sarcoidosis asks what caused her lung disease. Which response is accurate?","choices":["A medicine she has taken","Its cause is unknown","Inhaled asbestos fibers","An inherited single gene"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Sarcoidosis is a multi-organ disease of idiopathic cause, one of the interstitial lung diseases of unknown etiology with idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis. It is thought to arise from environmental triggers, such as infections, inhaled agents or mold, in a genetically predisposed person, but its etiology remains unknown. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 12, 14, 39, 40, 55"}]};
